 eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Yes, cookies protecting APIs is unfo=
rtunately still a thing. Whether it=E2=80=99s the protected resource sharin=
g the same cookie as the client application, or prompting the user to log i=
nto the protected resource before in order to use it as seen above.</p><p i=
d=3D"075a" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lb lc ev ld b le lf lg lh li lj =
lk ll lm ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv lw lx ly eo bj" data-selectable-paragra=
ph=3D"">So, you log into your API and get a cookie. Great, now the client a=
pplication can make requests. But if you open another tab in your browser a=
nd make a request manually to the API, you will still be successful. The pr=
oblem here is that we are no longer authorizing the application to access o=
ur API, instead we are authorizing the browser. This opens up a world of pa=
in in the form of <strong class=3D"ld ew">Cross Site Request Forgery</stron=
g> (CSRF or XSRF), where other applications will happily start using your A=
PI without your permission. Not good.</p><p id=3D"b354" class=3D"pw-post-bo=
dy-paragraph lb lc ev ld b le lf lg lh li lj lk ll lm ln lo lp lq lr ls lt =
lu lv lw lx ly eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">We also do not have mu=
ch of a revocation path, as now we=E2=80=99d need to invalidate a cookie st=
ored in a user=E2=80=99s browser by either waiting for them to make another=
 request, or by changing our keys used to protect the cookie, invalidating =
every cookie for that protected resource.</p><p id=3D"41b0" class=3D"pw-pos=
t-body-paragraph lb lc ev ld b le lf lg lh li lj lk ll lm ln lo lp lq lr ls=
 lt lu lv lw lx ly eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">I=E2=80=99m sure t=
here are hacks around some of these issues, but the point still stands, coo=
kies for APIs is a bad idea as they will always be open to CSRF.</p><h1 id=
=3D"cda9" class=3D"mk ml ev be mm mn mo mp mq mr ms mt mu mv mw mx my mz na=
 nb nc nd ne nf ng nh bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Summary</h1><p id=
=3D"6da8" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lb lc ev ld b le nu lg lh li nv l=
k ll lm nw lo lp lq nx ls lt lu ny lw lx ly eo bj" data-selectable-paragrap=
h=3D"">Most of the above involves the protected resource being aware of use=
r credentials and/or client credentials in some way. This isn=E2=80=99t unn=
ecessary and doesn=E2=80=99t scale well, as the more APIs you create, the m=
ore manual one-to-one integrations you have to implement. The obvious solut=
ion would be to create a central authorization server and have your APIs tr=
ust this to make authorization and delegation decisions, which is exactly w=
hat OAuth does.</p><p id=3D"d3bc" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lb lc ev =
ld b le lf lg lh li lj lk ll lm ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv lw lx ly eo bj" =
